# Ternary Operator

The ternary operator is a shorter form of `if...else`.

Syntax

```javascript
condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;
```

---

## Example

Instead of writing

```javascript
let age = 20;

let status;

if (age >= 18) {

    status = "Adult";

} else {

    status = "Minor";

}
```

You can write

```javascript
const age = 20;

const status = age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor";

console.log(status);
```

Output

```
Adult
```

---

# When Should You Use It?

Good

```javascript
const role = isAdmin ? "Admin" : "User";
```

Avoid

```javascript
const result =
condition1
? value1
: condition2
? value2
: condition3
? value3
: value4;
```

Nested ternary operators quickly become difficult to read.

---

# Expressions vs Statements

Many developers confuse these.

---

## Statement

A statement performs an action.

Example

```javascript
if (age > 18) {

    console.log("Adult");

}
```

---

## Expression

An expression produces a value.

Example

```javascript
age > 18
```

Produces

```
true

or

false
```

Another example

```javascript
10 + 20
```

Produces

```
30
```

The ternary operator is also an expression because it returns a value.

---

# Logical Assignment Operators

Modern JavaScript introduced assignment operators based on logical operators.

---

## OR Assignment (||=)

Old style

```javascript
if (!username) {

    username = "Guest";

}
```

Modern style

```javascript
username ||= "Guest";
```

If `username` is falsy, `"Guest"` is assigned.

---

## AND Assignment (&&=)

Example

```javascript
isLoggedIn &&= hasPermission;
```

This assigns a new value only if the left side is truthy.

You'll see this occasionally in modern JavaScript, but it's less common than `||=`.

